  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1240 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nBy Saturday night, an upper level short wave is forecast to move \nacross the Rockies and then over the Southern Plains on Sunday. At \nthe surface, a dry-line sits over the Pecos areas, however, it is \novertaken by a Pacific cold front late Saturday night. This frontal \nboundary then pushes into the southeast into the Rio Grande \novernight and exits the Coastal Plains Sunday afternoon. Rain \nchances of 20 to 40 percent are forecast for this period. Dry and \ncool air is then follows the front with Sunday's highs in the low to \nmid 60s across the Hill Country to mid 70s across the Coastal \nPlains. Partly cloudy to areas of clear skies are forecast for \nSunday night into Monday morning and light winds for some and others \naround 5 to 10 mph resulting in overnight lows in the upper 30s for \nthe Hill Country and 40s elsewhere. \n\nA cool start of the work week is in store with Monday's highs in the \n60s areawide. Stays cool on Tuesday with highs in the 60s. Another \nbut stronger and dry frontal boundary is forecast to push across \nthe local area between Tuesday night and Wednesday morning. This \ndry front brings a cooler airmass across the local area with \nWednesday's highs in the 50s and 60s. Under clear skies and light \nwinds, the temperatures Wednesday night into Thursday morning are \nforecast to drop to the mid 30s to low 40s. Can't rule out \nisolated spots along the river valleys reaching the freezing mark.\n\nThursday is looking to be a cool one with highs in the 50s to low \n60s across portions of the Coastal Plains and Rio Grande.\n\nRain chances are back on Friday (20 percent across most of South \nCentral Texas) as increased moisture and southerly wind flow returns \nand an upper level storm system drops from Arizona into our area.",
